        "original_id": "29ffef2a-c242-50fe-9f1d-4de9da90228d",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Ray Stark, Stanley Kubrick, Eliot Hyman\n- Companies: Seven Arts Productions\n- Films: The World of Suzie Wong, West Side Story, The Misfits, Lolita, The Night of the Iguana, Reflections in a Golden Eye, Funny Girl, The Goodbye Girl, The Toy, Annie, Steel Magnolias\n\nFacts:\n- Seven Arts Productions was founded in 1957 by Ray Stark and Eliot Hyman.\n- Ray Stark had a background as a literary and theatrical agent before becoming a film producer.\n- In 1961, Ray Stark produced The World of Suzie Wong, West Side Story, and The Misfits.\n- Lolita is a 1962 British-American black comedy-drama film directed by Stanley Kubrick.\n- In 1962, Ray Stark produced Lolita.\n- Ray Stark produced The Night of the Iguana in 1964, Reflections in a Golden Eye in 1967, and Funny Girl in 1968.\n- Ray Stark produced The Goodbye Girl in 1977, The Toy and Annie in 1982, and Steel Magnolias in 1989.\n- Ray Stark was regarded as highly tenacious and intelligent, and his producing career included dynamic and profitable films across the 1960s, 1970s, 1980s, and 1990s.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5f670f49-4331-5b32-9907-8ef69df2e848",
        "text": "Page: Ray Stark. Sentence: Highly tenacious and intelligent, Stark’s background as a literary and theatrical agent groomed him to produce some of the most dynamic and profitable films of the 1960s, 1970s, 1980s, and 1990s, such as \"The World of Suzie Wong\" (1961), \"West Side Story\" (1961), \"The Misfits\" (1961), \"Lolita\" (1962), \"The Night of The Iguana\" (1964), \"Reflections in a Golden Eye\" (1967), \"Funny Girl\" (1968), \"The Goodbye Girl\" (1977), \"The Toy\" (1982), \"Annie\" (1982), and \"Steel Magnolias\" (1989).\nPage: Lolita (1962 film). Sentence: Lolita is a 1962 British-American black comedy-drama film directed by Stanley Kubrick.\nPage: Seven Arts Productions. Sentence: It was founded in 1957 by Ray Stark and Eliot Hyman.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
